WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
The two building-block categories most relevant to TLS migration are key establishment and

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:05.739
digital signatures.

00:00:05.739 --> 00:00:07.890
A key encapsulation mechanism, or KEM,

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:12.160
helps two parties establish shared secret material over an untrusted channel.

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:16.180
In post-quantum planning, ML-KEM is the main KEM concept teams will encounter.

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:18.270
It is intended for key establishment use cases,

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:22.020
not for signing certificates or authenticating software by itself.

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:23.760
Digital signatures solve a different problem.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.785
They help a verifier confirm that data was signed by the holder of a private key and that

00:00:27.785 --> 00:00:29.275
the signed data has not changed.

00:00:29.275 --> 00:00:34.815
ML-DSA and SLH-DSA are post-quantum digital signature concepts practitioners may see in

00:00:34.815 --> 00:00:36.200
discussions about certificates,

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:40.315
code signing, identity, service authentication, and long-term verification.

00:00:40.315 --> 00:00:44.080
They are not interchangeable with KEMs because they answer a different security question.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:45.520
That distinction matters for TLS.

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:49.415
A deployment may experiment with post-quantum or hybrid key establishment before the

00:00:49.415 --> 00:00:53.380
broader certificate ecosystem is ready for post-quantum signatures.

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:56.980
Post-quantum certificates and certificate chains introduce separate compatibility

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:57.440
concerns,

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:02.255
such as signature size, chain size, client support, certificate authority readiness,

00:01:02.255 --> 00:01:05.150
browser behavior, operating system trust, embedded devices,

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:07.520
and enterprise inspection tools.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:10.060
One migration track does not automatically solve the other.

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:13.840
Teams should use standards-based algorithms through mature libraries and protocols.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:18.175
Custom TLS cryptography is dangerous because security depends on more than the algorithm

00:01:18.175 --> 00:01:18.920
name.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:22.590
It depends on negotiation behavior, transcript binding, downgrade resistance,

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:26.830
error handling, implementation quality, side-channel considerations, interoperability,

00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:27.760
and maintenance.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:32.440
The practical advice is simple: do not design private post-quantum TLS constructions.

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:35.025
Track reputable standards work, use maintained libraries,

00:01:35.025 --> 00:01:36.680
and test in realistic environments.
